<Article slug="after-hours-problem-hvac">
  <p>
    If you have read anything about missed calls in the trades, you have probably seen the claim that 35 to 45% of HVAC
    call volume arrives after hours. We repeated it ourselves, until we found the platform data. It is wrong by a factor
    of three or four. The real leak in a small shop is less dramatic and more expensive: the calls that go unanswered
    during the working day, while the owner is on a roof.
  </p>

  <h2>The three numbers that define the problem</h2>
  <p>
    <b>After hours is 10 to 14% of calls, not 40%.</b> ServiceTitan's platform data on residential HVAC contractors
    through 2025 puts off-hours calls, outside Monday to Friday 8am to 5pm, at 14.1% at the June peak and 9.8% at the
    October trough. Those calls matter, because a no-heat call at 11pm is often the most valuable job of the week. But
    they are not most of the problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Nearly half of callers never reach a person.</b> Invoca's 2026 benchmarks, drawn from more than 70 million tracked
    calls, found that only 52% of callers to home-service businesses spoke with a person. Answer rates vary widely by
    trade, from 32% to 74%. If only a small fraction of calls arrive after hours and nearly half go unanswered, most of
    the unanswered ones are arriving during business hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Most callers do not wait for you.</b> In a CallRail survey of 1,000 US consumers, 82% said they would call a
    competitor if a business did not answer, and only 42% said they leave a voicemail. Those are stated intentions rather
    than observed behaviour, but they point the same way as everything else. Your marketing paid for that lead and a
    competitor closed it.
  </p>

  <h2>What one missed call is worth</h2>
  <p>
    Service Roundtable benchmarks, as reported by the AI phone vendor AInora, put the blended average revenue per booked
    residential HVAC call at $450 to $950, with a typical booking rate around 65%. That makes the expected value of a
    single missed call roughly $290 at the bottom of the range: $450 times a 65% chance it would have booked.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will see much bigger numbers quoted, often $1,200 to $3,500 per missed call. We could not trace those to any
    underlying data, so we do not use them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why voicemail stopped working</h2>
  <p>
    Voicemail was fine when the alternative was also voicemail. It stopped working when a meaningful share of your
    competitors started answering. A customer with a failing furnace in February is not leaving a considered message
    and waiting; they are working down a list until someone picks up. The first shop to answer books the job, and often
    the relationship.
  </p>
  <p>
    The traditional fix, a human answering service, has its own problem: they take a message. They do not know your
    service area, your job durations, your on-call rotation, or whether the caller is an existing customer with a
    system you installed two years ago. So you still wake up to a list of callbacks.
  </p>

  <h2>What a realistic fix looks like</h2>
  <p>Whatever you buy, it needs to do four things, in this order of importance:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <b>Recognise a genuine emergency and page a human immediately.</b> No heat with an elderly customer, gas smell,
      burst pipe, electrical burning. These cannot wait for a morning summary, and any system that treats them as
      routine is worse than voicemail.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Book real jobs into real slots.</b> Checking actual calendar availability, respecting your service area, using
      your job-type durations. A booking that you have to rearrange in the morning is not a booking.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Capture quote requests properly.</b> Job details, photos where possible, enough for you to price it without
      calling back to ask basics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>Never invent a price.</b> Quoting from a published price book is fine. Improvising is how you end up honouring
      a number you would never have given.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Notice that none of these are specific to nights. A system that only switches on at 5pm misses the daytime calls
    that make up most of the leak.
  </p>
  <p>
    Vendors in this category publish large improvements in missed-call rates and dispatch speed after shops switch from
    a traditional answering service. We are not going to quote a specific number, because every figure we could find
    traces back to a company selling the software, and we have found no independent study of AI phone agents in the
    trades. Measure it on your own shop rather than trusting anyone's marketing page, including ours.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <b>The arithmetic for a two-truck shop:</b> 60 calls in a busy week, 40% missed, 24 unanswered. At a 65% booking
      rate about 16 were bookable. Recover 8 to 10 a week at a $450 average ticket and that is $3,600 to $4,500 a week.
      HVAC is seasonal, so count 26 to 40 weeks a year at that volume: roughly $94,000 to $180,000 a year. Against a
      service costing $15,000 a year, you do not need the estimate to be accurate. You need it to be within an order of
      magnitude.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Check your own number first</h2>
  <p>
    Before believing any of this, pull your call log for the last 30 days. Most VoIP systems export answered versus
    missed, with timestamps. Split it into business hours, evenings and weekends, and see where your misses actually
    fall. Multiply the missed count by your own booking rate and your own average ticket. You now have your actual
    number, which is worth more than any benchmark in this article.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/home-services#calc">home services calculator</a> will do the arithmetic if you would rather not.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources, and a caveat about them</h2>
  <p>
    Worth stating plainly: the home services trade has no equivalent of Clio's Legal Trends Report. The best data comes
    from software platforms reporting on their own customers' calls, and much of what circulates beyond that originates
    from companies selling answering software, often citing each other rather than any primary data. We have removed
    the figures we could not trace, and we say who published each one we kept.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.servicetitan.com/toolbox/state-of-the-trades/trends/hvac-summer-after-hours-call-spike" rel="nofollow noopener">ServiceTitan, HVAC after-hours call data, 2025</a> for the off-hours share. ServiceTitan sells field-service software.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.invoca.com/reports/the-invoca-home-services-lead-conversion-benchmarks-report-2026" rel="nofollow noopener">Invoca Home Services Lead Conversion Benchmarks Report, 2026</a> for answer rates. Invoca sells call-tracking software.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.callrail.com/blog/missed-calls-cost-businesses-more-than-ever" rel="nofollow noopener">CallRail consumer survey, 2025</a> for caller behaviour. CallRail sells call-tracking software.</li>
    <li><a href="https://ainora.lt/blog/hvac-service-call-statistics-2026" rel="nofollow noopener">AInora, HVAC, Plumbing and Electrical Service Call Statistics, 2026</a>, reporting Service Roundtable benchmarks for ticket value and booking rate. AInora sells AI phone software.</li>
    <li><a href="https://calljolt.com/blog/hvac/home-service-industry-phone-statistics" rel="nofollow noopener">CallJolt, Home Service Phone Statistics, 2026</a> for the vendor-reported improvement range we decline to quote.</li>
  </ul>
</Article>
